#!/usr/bin/env bash
# scripts/gate.sh — the project's quality gate, in CI order. SINGLE SOURCE.
#
# This is project-OWNED config, not harness machinery: it encodes THIS project's
# formatter / linter / type-checker / test runner. PROMPT.md, the pre-commit hook
# (hooks/pre-commit), and CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml) all invoke this one file —
# the gate command must never be duplicated, or the three will drift.
#
# Contract: run every check in the SAME order CI runs them; exit 0 only if all
# pass; exit non-zero on the first failure. `set -e` enforces that with `&&`-free
# sequencing — each command must succeed for the next to run.
#
# PLACEHOLDERS (the closed set):
#   {{GATE_COMMAND}}  the full gate, in CI order, as one line. It MUST include a
#                     test-COVERAGE check with a threshold, not just a test run
#                     (e.g. `ruff format . && ruff check . && mypy . &&
#                      pytest --cov=<pkg> --cov-fail-under=80`).
#
# Run a FORMATTER, not just a format-CHECK: a passing-tests commit still fails CI
# if it is unformatted.
#
# Gate COVERAGE, not just test pass/fail: "tests pass" is not "the code is tested."
# Two modes — pick one to confirm with /ralph-init:
#
#   GLOBAL floor (default for GREENFIELD): the project-wide % must meet the
#   threshold. Portable across tools, fits a project grown test-first. E.g.
#     pytest --cov=<pkg> --cov-fail-under=80
#
#   PATCH/SCOPED (recommended for BROWNFIELD — adding a feature to an existing,
#   under-covered codebase): gate only the code this work touches, so legacy
#   untested modules don't tank the number and fail your first commit. Scope to
#   the new paths (simplest, tool-native):
#     Python   pytest --cov=<new_pkg_or_path> --cov-fail-under=80
#     vitest   vitest run --coverage --coverage.include='<feature-dir>/**' \
#                --coverage.thresholds.lines=80
#     go       go test -cover ./<feature-pkg>/...
#   Or true diff coverage against the base branch (stricter, needs a tool + base
#   ref): generate an lcov/coverage.xml report, then `diff-cover` it.
#
# Coverage is a SUPPORTING gate — it catches lazy/trivial tests, but not tests that
# fake the precondition that matters; the independent-review gate catches that class
# and is also the backstop for scoped coverage's blind spot (changes outside the
# scoped path).

set -euo pipefail

{{GATE_COMMAND}}
